<section class="panel" aria-label="Addition? No: analyzing Grade 3 subtraction algorithms">
<h2>Warm-up — Number Talk: Subtract within 1,000</h2>
<p>Calculate mentally, one at a time, then explain how the last two differ.</p>
<ol><li>400 − 200</li><li>450 − 200</li><li>450 − 205</li><li>450 − 215</li></ol>
<h2>Activity 1 — Compare Two Subtraction Algorithms</h2>
<p>The original source asks you to compare different starting steps. Here is an independently created text representation of the same place-value methods; the original publisher diagram is not copied.</p>
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:12px">
<div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;padding:12px;border-radius:8px"><h3>Method A: exchange before subtracting</h3><p>824 = 8 hundreds + 2 tens + 4 ones.</p><p>Trade a hundred: 7 hundreds + 12 tens + 4 ones.</p><p>Subtract 541 to get 2 hundreds + 8 tens + 3 ones = 283.</p></div>
<div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;padding:12px;border-radius:8px"><h3>Method B: exchange only as needed</h3><p>824 − 541.</p><p>4 ones − 1 one = 3 ones.</p><p>Not enough tens? Exchange one hundred, giving 12 tens.</p><p>12 tens − 4 tens = 8 tens; 7 hundreds − 5 hundreds = 2 hundreds.</p></div></div>
<p>With your partner, compare the first steps, then finish both approaches. State one advantage of each (MLR8). The teacher reviews the original diagram and discussion, not a numeric-only score.</p>
<h2>Activity 2 — Use an Algorithm?</h2>
<p>Elena thinks 301 − 167 may be impossible because there are 0 tens and only 1 one. Why is this false?</p>
<div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;padding:12px;border-radius:8px" role="img" aria-label="An independent exchange chain showing 301 as 3 hundreds 0 tens 1 one, then 2 hundreds 10 tens 1 one, and finally 2 hundreds 9 tens 11 ones">
<p>3 hundreds · 0 tens · 1 one</p><p>↓ exchange 1 hundred into 10 tens</p><p>2 hundreds · 10 tens · 1 one</p><p>↓ exchange 1 ten into 10 ones</p><p>2 hundreds · 9 tens · 11 ones</p>
</div>
<p>Subtract 1 hundred, 6 tens and 7 ones. Explain the resulting 134 and why both exchanges conserve the number 301.</p>
<h2>Synthesis and original source</h2><p>When would you decompose before starting, and when would you decompose only when needed? The teacher assesses the reasoning and original image comparison. Edulytics independently checks the same subtraction skills in its own practice and Cool-down.</p>
